{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan-review routing clarification on current base 2d440ad668: this subject IS story 4a44, the flag owner explicitly named by prerequisite 4752. The current absence of max-changes, only, and except is the implementation gap this forward-looking story closes, not an assertion that those capabilities already exist. Exact canonical wiring: _bridge_commands._parser adds options to preview and sync; bridge_cli serializes the parsed selection and cap into engine argv; _launch_bridge_command passes (command, plus serialized child options) to launch_reconciler with no injected legacy default; launch_reconciler injects --mode only for the retained legacy reconcile adapter and otherwise forwards the canonical preview or sync subcommand unchanged. Engine request.py parses both shapes into one normalized request before lock acquisition. Thus bridge sync --max-changes 10 reaches request.max_changes=10 while legacy reconcile --mode bootstrap-strict still reaches Mode.BOOTSTRAP_STRICT and its existing MODE_CAPS behavior. The workflow changes only after this parser, forwarding, normalization, partition, and manifest behavior land in the same Gerrit change, so it never relies on an already-existing command. The parent epic explicitly requires all four surviving operational flags, including except; except is therefore a committed epic contract and is proven through the real CLI even though no old caller can use a flag that does not yet exist."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786221128208722001,"uuid":"0cc0b1b4-6501-450c-97a5-1ceebadf14e7"}